window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["restful-api"]=[{"id":"restful-api-f0001","card":"q-restful-api-001-1-course-overview-and-the-planetary-api","sec":"restful-api-s002","q":"What does it mean that Flask is a microframework, and what is the trade-off compared with Django?","a":"<p>Flask gives you routing, request/response handling and a template engine, and leaves the rest (database layer, auth, forms, admin) to extensions you choose. That keeps it small and flexible and suits APIs and small services. The trade-off is that you pick and wire the pieces yourself, while Django ships an ORM, auth and admin out of the box and enforces more structure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0002","card":"q-restful-api-001-1-course-overview-and-the-planetary-api","sec":"restful-api-s002","q":"Why do REST APIs usually return JSON rather than XML or a custom text format?","a":"<p>JSON is light, maps directly to dicts, lists, strings and numbers, and every mainstream language parses it natively. XML is more verbose and needs heavier parsing, and a custom format forces every client to write its own parser.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0003","card":"q-restful-api-001-1-course-overview-and-the-planetary-api","sec":"restful-api-s002","q":"Why is SQLite a reasonable database for development and small projects, and when would you move off it?","a":"<p>SQLite is a single file with no server to install or manage, and it ships with Python, so setup is trivial. Move to PostgreSQL or MySQL when you need many concurrent writers, network access from several app servers, finer user permissions, or larger scale, because SQLite locks the whole database file for writes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0004","card":"q-restful-api-001-1-course-overview-and-the-planetary-api","sec":"restful-api-s002","q":"Why build a backend-only REST API with no UI?","a":"<p>A UI-less API is client-agnostic. The same endpoints serve a web front end, a mobile app and a desktop app, and you can test them with a tool such as Postman or curl without any front end.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0005","card":"q-restful-api-003-2-project-setup-first-endpoint-dev-serve","sec":"restful-api-s004","q":"In a Flask app, what do <code>Flask(__name__)</code> and <code>@app.route('/')</code> each do?","a":"<p><code>Flask(__name__)</code> creates the application object. The <code>__name__</code> argument tells Flask where the module lives so it can locate resources such as templates and static files. <code>@app.route('/')</code> registers the decorated function as the handler for that URL path, so a request to <code>/</code> calls it and its return value becomes the response.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0006","card":"q-restful-api-003-2-project-setup-first-endpoint-dev-serve","sec":"restful-api-s004","q":"Why use a virtual environment for a Flask project?","a":"<p>It isolates the project's packages and versions from the system Python and from other projects. Dependencies stay reproducible (for example through <code>pip freeze &gt; requirements.txt</code>) and you avoid version conflicts between projects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0007","card":"q-restful-api-003-2-project-setup-first-endpoint-dev-serve","sec":"restful-api-s004","q":"Why is the <code>if __name__ == '__main__':</code> guard used around <code>app.run()</code>?","a":"<p>It runs the server only when the file is executed directly. When the module is imported (by a test, a WSGI server or the Flask CLI), the guard stops it from starting a second dev server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0008","card":"q-restful-api-004-running-and-testing","sec":"restful-api-s005","q":"Why is Flask's built-in development server not suitable for production, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>It is the Werkzeug dev server. It is meant for local development, is not hardened, and does not handle concurrency, process management or TLS well. In production run the app under a WSGI server such as Gunicorn or uWSGI, usually behind a reverse proxy such as Nginx.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0009","card":"q-restful-api-004-running-and-testing","sec":"restful-api-s005","q":"Why test an API with Postman or curl instead of a browser address bar?","a":"<p>A browser address bar can only send GET. A CRUD API also needs POST, PUT and DELETE, custom headers (for example <code>Authorization</code>) and request bodies. Postman and curl let you set the verb, headers and body, and show the status code, response headers and body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0010","card":"q-restful-api-004-running-and-testing","sec":"restful-api-s005","q":"How do you get automatic reload and the debugger when developing a Flask app?","a":"<p>Turn on debug mode. In Flask 2.2 and later use <code>flask --app app run --debug</code>, or set <code>FLASK_DEBUG=1</code>. In older Flask you set <code>FLASK_ENV=development</code>. <code>FLASK_ENV</code> was deprecated in Flask 2.2 and removed in 2.3. Debug mode restarts the server when code changes and shows an interactive debugger on errors, so never enable it in production because the debugger allows code execution.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0011","card":"q-restful-api-004-running-and-testing","sec":"restful-api-s005","q":"Where should configuration such as database URLs and API keys live, and why?","a":"<p>In environment variables or a config file kept out of version control, read at startup (for example with <code>os.environ</code>). That keeps secrets out of the repository and lets the same code run in development, test and production with different values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0012","card":"q-restful-api-005-jsonify","sec":"restful-api-s006","q":"What does Flask's <code>jsonify</code> do that returning a plain string or <code>str(dict)</code> does not?","a":"<p><code>jsonify</code> serializes the data to valid JSON and builds a response with <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>. A plain string is sent as <code>text/html</code>, and <code>str(dict)</code> produces Python repr with single quotes, which is not valid JSON, so clients cannot parse it reliably.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0013","card":"q-restful-api-005-jsonify","sec":"restful-api-s006","q":"What does this return?","a":"<p>A 200 response with the body <code>{\"code\": 1, \"message\": \"ok\"}</code> and <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>. Keyword arguments become the JSON keys. Key order in the output is not something to rely on.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask import Flask, jsonify\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(&#x27;/status&#x27;)\ndef status():\n    return jsonify(message=&quot;ok&quot;, code=1)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0014","card":"q-restful-api-005-jsonify","sec":"restful-api-s006","q":"In Flask, can a view return a dict directly instead of calling <code>jsonify</code>?","a":"<p>Yes, in Flask 1.1 and later a view can return a dict (and in 2.2 and later a list), and Flask converts it to JSON. <code>jsonify</code> is still useful on older versions and when you want to build the response explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0015","card":"q-restful-api-006-http-status-codes","sec":"restful-api-s007","q":"What do the 2xx, 4xx and 5xx status code classes mean, and why does the distinction matter to a client?","a":"<p>2xx means the request succeeded, 4xx means the client made a mistake (bad input, missing auth, not found) and retrying the same request will not help, 5xx means the server failed and a retry may work later. Clients branch on these classes to decide whether to show an error, re-authenticate or retry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0016","card":"q-restful-api-006-http-status-codes","sec":"restful-api-s007","q":"How do you return a non-200 status code from a Flask view?","a":"<p>Return a tuple of body and status, for example <code>return jsonify(message=\"not found\"), 404</code>. A view that returns only a body gets 200 by default. You can also add headers as a third tuple element.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0017","card":"q-restful-api-006-http-status-codes","sec":"restful-api-s007","q":"What is the difference between 401 and 403?","a":"<p>401 Unauthorized means the request has no valid credentials (no token, bad or expired token), so authenticate and try again. 403 Forbidden means the caller is authenticated but not allowed to do this action. Using 401 for a failed login or missing token and 403 for a permissions failure is the usual convention.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0018","card":"q-restful-api-006-http-status-codes","sec":"restful-api-s007","q":"What is the difference between 400 and 404 when a client sends a bad request to an endpoint?","a":"<p>400 Bad Request means the request itself is malformed or invalid, such as a missing field or a non-numeric value where a number is required. 404 Not Found means the URL or the addressed resource does not exist. A well-formed request for an ID that does not exist gets 404.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0019","card":"q-restful-api-007-url-query-parameters","sec":"restful-api-s008","q":"In Flask, how do you read <code>?name=Bruce&amp;age=28</code> in a view, and what type does each value have?","a":"<p>Use <code>request.args.get('name')</code>, where <code>request</code> is imported from <code>flask</code>. Every value is a string, so convert numbers yourself, for example <code>int(request.args.get('age'))</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0020","card":"q-restful-api-007-url-query-parameters","sec":"restful-api-s008","q":"What goes wrong in this view when a client calls <code>/check</code> with no <code>age</code> parameter or with <code>age=abc</code>, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>A missing <code>age</code> makes <code>get</code> return <code>None</code>, so <code>int(None)</code> raises <code>TypeError</code>. <code>abc</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>. Both end as a 500 error. Validate first and return 400 with a clear message. Another fix is <code>request.args.get('age', type=int)</code>, which returns <code>None</code> when conversion fails, so you still check for <code>None</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask import Flask, jsonify, request\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(&#x27;/check&#x27;)\ndef check():\n    age = int(request.args.get(&#x27;age&#x27;))\n    return jsonify(adult=age &gt;= 18)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0021","card":"q-restful-api-007-url-query-parameters","sec":"restful-api-s008","q":"When do you choose query parameters over path variables in a REST API?","a":"<p>Use path variables to identify a resource (<code>/books/42</code>). Use query parameters for optional input that filters, sorts, searches or paginates a collection (<code>/books?author=x&amp;page=2</code>). Query parameters are optional and unordered, while path segments are part of the resource identity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0022","card":"q-restful-api-007-url-query-parameters","sec":"restful-api-s008","q":"Why must a query parameter be validated and sanitized before it reaches a database query?","a":"<p>Query values are untrusted client input. Unvalidated, they cause type errors and, if concatenated into SQL, SQL injection. Validate type and range, and use an ORM or parameterized queries so the value is bound as data and never becomes SQL text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0023","card":"q-restful-api-008-url-variables-and-conversion-filters","sec":"restful-api-s009","q":"In Flask routing, what does <code>&lt;int:age&gt;</code> do, and what happens if the client sends <code>/user/abc</code>?","a":"<p>It captures that path segment, converts it to an <code>int</code> and passes it to the view argument named <code>age</code>. If the segment is not an integer the route does not match and Flask returns 404 without calling the view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0024","card":"q-restful-api-008-url-variables-and-conversion-filters","sec":"restful-api-s009","q":"Which built-in Flask URL converters exist and what does each accept?","a":"<p><code>string</code> (the default) accepts any text without slashes. <code>int</code> accepts positive integers. <code>float</code> accepts positive floating-point values. <code>path</code> is like <code>string</code> but also accepts slashes. <code>uuid</code> accepts UUID strings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0025","card":"q-restful-api-008-url-variables-and-conversion-filters","sec":"restful-api-s009","q":"What is wrong with this route, and where does the converter go compared with a type hint?","a":"<p>The view parameter <code>person</code> does not match the route variable <code>name</code>. Flask passes variables as keyword arguments, so it raises a <code>TypeError</code>. Names must match exactly. The converter belongs in the route before the variable name (<code>&lt;int:age&gt;</code>). A type hint belongs in the function signature (<code>age: int</code>), and Flask ignores the hint, so the converter does the conversion.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask import Flask, jsonify\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(&#x27;/users/&lt;name&gt;/&lt;int:age&gt;&#x27;)\ndef users(person, age):\n    return jsonify(name=person, age=age)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0026","card":"q-restful-api-008-url-variables-and-conversion-filters","sec":"restful-api-s009","q":"When would you use the <code>path</code> converter?","a":"<p>When one variable must capture a value that contains slashes, such as a file path or a nested resource key (<code>/files/&lt;path:filepath&gt;</code>). The default <code>string</code> stops at the first slash.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0027","card":"q-restful-api-009-4-database-with-sqlalchemy-models-and-se","sec":"restful-api-s010","q":"What problem does an ORM such as SQLAlchemy solve, and what is the cost?","a":"<p>It maps tables to Python classes and rows to objects, and builds SQL for you. Benefits: no hand-written SQL strings, parameter binding that protects against SQL injection, the schema defined in code under version control, and easier switching between databases such as SQLite and PostgreSQL. Costs: an extra abstraction layer, harder tuning of complex queries, and the risk of inefficient patterns such as N+1 queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0028","card":"q-restful-api-009-4-database-with-sqlalchemy-models-and-se","sec":"restful-api-s010","q":"Which package do you install to use SQLAlchemy with Flask, and what does it add over plain SQLAlchemy?","a":"<p><code>Flask-SQLAlchemy</code> (pip name with a dash). It integrates SQLAlchemy with the Flask app: configuration through <code>app.config</code>, a managed session (<code>db.session</code>) and a <code>db.Model</code> base class. It installs <code>SQLAlchemy</code> as a dependency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0029","card":"q-restful-api-009-4-database-with-sqlalchemy-models-and-se","sec":"restful-api-s010","q":"In a Flask app using Flask-SQLAlchemy, what is <code>SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI</code> and why is <code>os.path.join</code> used when building it for SQLite?","a":"<p>It is the config key that holds the connection URL. The key name must be spelled exactly, or the extension falls back to defaults or raises an error. For SQLite the URL is <code>sqlite:///</code> followed by a file path. <code>os.path.join</code> builds the path with the right separator for the operating system, and <code>basedir</code> makes the file location independent of the working directory. The extension reads the config when <code>SQLAlchemy(app)</code> runs, so set the key first.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import os\nfrom flask import Flask\nfrom flask_sqlalchemy import SQLAlchemy\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\nbasedir = os.path.abspath(os.path.dirname(__file__))\napp.config[&#x27;SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI&#x27;] = &#x27;sqlite:///&#x27; + os.path.join(basedir, &#x27;app.db&#x27;)\ndb = SQLAlchemy(app)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0030","card":"q-restful-api-010-orm-models","sec":"restful-api-s011","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, how do you define a table as a model, and what do <code>__tablename__</code> and <code>db.Column</code> do?","a":"<p>A model is a class inheriting <code>db.Model</code>. <code>__tablename__</code> sets the table name (otherwise Flask-SQLAlchemy derives one from the class name). Each <code>db.Column</code> becomes a column with a type, and <code>primary_key=True</code> marks the identifier, which the database auto-increments for an integer key. <code>unique=True</code> adds a unique constraint, so a duplicate <code>isbn</code> makes the commit fail with an integrity error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Book(db.Model):\n    __tablename__ = &#x27;books&#x27;\n    id = db.Column(db.Integer, primary_key=True)\n    title = db.Column(db.String)\n    isbn = db.Column(db.String, unique=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0031","card":"q-restful-api-010-orm-models","sec":"restful-api-s011","q":"What is the difference between defining a uniqueness check in code and a <code>unique=True</code> constraint in the column?","a":"<p>A code check (query first, then insert) is a race: two concurrent requests can both pass it. The database constraint is enforced atomically, so it is the real guarantee. Use both: the code check gives a friendly error, and the constraint guarantees integrity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0032","card":"q-restful-api-010-orm-models","sec":"restful-api-s011","q":"Why should a column holding passwords never be a plain <code>db.String</code> filled with the raw password?","a":"<p>If the database leaks, every password is exposed, and users reuse passwords elsewhere. Store a salted hash made with a slow password-hashing function (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2, or <code>werkzeug.security.generate_password_hash</code>) and compare with the matching check function.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0033","card":"q-restful-api-010-orm-models","sec":"restful-api-s011","q":"How is a model class different from a schema class in a Flask API with Flask-SQLAlchemy and Marshmallow?","a":"<p>The model describes the database table and its ORM mapping. The schema describes how an object is converted to and from JSON (which fields, validation). Keeping them separate lets you hide columns such as password hashes and change the API shape without changing the table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0034","card":"q-restful-api-011-flask-cli-commands-create-drop-seed","sec":"restful-api-s012","q":"How do you register a custom command that you run as <code>flask db_create</code>, and why use it for database setup?","a":"<p><code>@app.cli.command('name')</code> registers the function with the Flask CLI (built on Click), so <code>flask db_create</code> runs it inside the app context. That context is what lets <code>db.create_all()</code> find the app configuration. It makes setup, reset and seed tasks repeatable and scriptable without adding HTTP endpoints for them.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@app.cli.command(&#x27;db_create&#x27;)\ndef db_create():\n    db.create_all()\n    print(&#x27;Database created&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0035","card":"q-restful-api-011-flask-cli-commands-create-drop-seed","sec":"restful-api-s012","q":"What do <code>db.create_all()</code> and <code>db.drop_all()</code> do, and what are the limits of <code>create_all</code>?","a":"<p><code>create_all()</code> creates tables for all known models that do not exist yet. <code>drop_all()</code> drops them and deletes the data. <code>create_all()</code> does not alter existing tables, so adding a column to a model has no effect on an existing table. For schema changes in real projects use a migration tool such as Alembic through Flask-Migrate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0036","card":"q-restful-api-011-flask-cli-commands-create-drop-seed","sec":"restful-api-s012","q":"In SQLAlchemy sessions, what is the difference between <code>db.session.add()</code> and <code>db.session.commit()</code>, and what happens if you forget the commit?","a":"<p><code>add()</code> stages the object in the session, and <code>commit()</code> flushes the SQL to the database and ends the transaction. Without <code>commit()</code> the changes are rolled back when the session closes, so nothing is saved and the next request does not see them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0037","card":"q-restful-api-011-flask-cli-commands-create-drop-seed","sec":"restful-api-s012","q":"Why add several records and then commit once, instead of committing after each?","a":"<p>One commit means one transaction, so the batch is atomic (all rows are saved or none) and it is faster than a commit per row. If an error happens, call <code>db.session.rollback()</code> so the session stays usable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0038","card":"q-restful-api-011-flask-cli-commands-create-drop-seed","sec":"restful-api-s012","q":"Why seed a database from a CLI command rather than inserting rows by hand?","a":"<p>A seed command gives every developer and every test run the same known starting data, can be re-run after <code>drop_all</code> and <code>create_all</code>, and is version-controlled. Never put real credentials in seed data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0039","card":"q-restful-api-012-5-serialization-with-marshmallow","sec":"restful-api-s013","q":"In a Flask view, why does <code>jsonify(data=Item.query.all())</code> fail with \"Object of type Item is not JSON serializable\", and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>ORM objects are instances of your own class, and the JSON encoder only knows built-in types (dict, list, str, numbers, bool, None). Convert the objects to dicts first. A serialization library such as Marshmallow does this using a schema that lists which fields to include.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0040","card":"q-restful-api-012-5-serialization-with-marshmallow","sec":"restful-api-s013","q":"What is the role of Marshmallow in a Flask API, and why use it instead of building dicts in a loop?","a":"<p>Marshmallow serializes objects to JSON-compatible dicts (<code>dump</code>) and also deserializes and validates incoming data (<code>load</code>). A schema declares the fields once and is reused for single objects and lists, so you avoid repeated manual dict building and keep the output format in one place. Flask-Marshmallow adds Flask integration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0041","card":"q-restful-api-012-5-serialization-with-marshmallow","sec":"restful-api-s013","q":"In Marshmallow, what does <code>many=True</code> do?","a":"<p><code>many=True</code> tells the schema it will receive a collection and must serialize each element, returning a list. The single-object schema takes one object and returns one dict. Passing a list to the single schema, or one object to the <code>many</code> schema, gives wrong results or errors.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class ItemSchema(ma.Schema):\n    class Meta:\n        fields = (&#x27;id&#x27;, &#x27;name&#x27;)\n\nitem_schema = ItemSchema()\nitems_schema = ItemSchema(many=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0042","card":"q-restful-api-012-5-serialization-with-marshmallow","sec":"restful-api-s013","q":"Why does <code>Meta.fields</code> matter for security, and why not dump every column of a user model?","a":"<p><code>fields</code> is an allow-list of what leaves the API. Dumping every column leaks sensitive data such as password hashes or internal flags. List only safe fields explicitly, or use <code>exclude</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0043","card":"q-restful-api-012-5-serialization-with-marshmallow","sec":"restful-api-s013","q":"In Marshmallow 3 and later, how do you use the result of <code>dump</code>, compared with Marshmallow 2?","a":"<p>In Marshmallow 3 and later <code>schema.dump(obj)</code> returns the dict directly, so you pass it straight to <code>jsonify</code>. In Marshmallow 2 it returned a result object, and you read the dict from <code>.data</code>. Code that uses <code>.data</code> fails on version 3 with an <code>AttributeError</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0044","card":"q-restful-api-012-5-serialization-with-marshmallow","sec":"restful-api-s013","q":"In Flask-Marshmallow, why must the <code>Marshmallow(app)</code> object be created after the <code>SQLAlchemy(app)</code> object?","a":"<p>When both extensions are present, Flask-Marshmallow can build schemas from SQLAlchemy models (through <code>marshmallow-sqlalchemy</code>) only if SQLAlchemy is initialized first. Creating it after is the documented order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0045","card":"q-restful-api-013-6-crud-endpoints","sec":"restful-api-s014","q":"How do the CRUD operations map to HTTP verbs and typical success codes in a REST API?","a":"<p>Create is POST (201 Created), read is GET (200), update is PUT for full replacement or PATCH for partial change (200, or 204 with no body), and delete is DELETE (200 or 204). GET must be safe (no side effects), and PUT and DELETE should be idempotent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0046","card":"q-restful-api-013-6-crud-endpoints","sec":"restful-api-s014","q":"Why restrict a Flask route with <code>methods=['GET']</code> or <code>['POST']</code>?","a":"<p>By default a Flask route answers only GET (and HEAD and OPTIONS). Listing methods makes the contract explicit, and a request with another verb gets 405 Method Not Allowed instead of reaching code that does not expect it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0047","card":"q-restful-api-013-6-crud-endpoints","sec":"restful-api-s014","q":"Which endpoints in a typical API should be open and which need authentication, and how do you decide?","a":"<p>Reads of public data and account creation or login are usually open. Anything that changes data (create, update, delete) needs authentication, and often authorization for specific users or roles. Decide per endpoint by asking who may call it and what damage a malicious call would do.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0048","card":"q-restful-api-013-6-crud-endpoints","sec":"restful-api-s014","q":"Why is it sensible to build an endpoint without authentication first and add protection afterwards?","a":"<p>It separates two problems. You first confirm the logic and status codes with plain requests, and then add the auth decorator and test the new 401 behavior. A failure then clearly belongs to either the logic or the auth.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0049","card":"q-restful-api-014-read-one-planet-get","sec":"restful-api-s015","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what is the difference between <code>.first()</code> and <code>.all()</code> on a query, and what does <code>.first()</code> return when nothing matches?","a":"<p><code>.all()</code> returns a list of every match (empty if none). <code>.first()</code> returns the first match or <code>None</code>. Check for <code>None</code> before using the result, otherwise you get an <code>AttributeError</code> and a 500.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0050","card":"q-restful-api-014-read-one-planet-get","sec":"restful-api-s015","q":"Why does a \"get one\" endpoint return 404 when the ID does not exist, and what should the body contain?","a":"<p>404 tells the client the addressed resource is not there, as opposed to 400 for a bad request or 500 for a server fault. The body is a short JSON message so API clients can show or log it. Using <code>&lt;int:item_id&gt;</code> also means a non-integer ID gets a 404 from routing before the view runs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@app.route(&#x27;/items/&lt;int:item_id&gt;&#x27;, methods=[&#x27;GET&#x27;])\ndef item_details(item_id: int):\n    item = Item.query.filter_by(id=item_id).first()\n    if item:\n        return jsonify(item_schema.dump(item))\n    return jsonify(message=&quot;that item does not exist&quot;), 404</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0051","card":"q-restful-api-014-read-one-planet-get","sec":"restful-api-s015","q":"What is the difference between <code>filter_by(id=...)</code> and <code>Query.get(id)</code> in SQLAlchemy?","a":"<p><code>filter_by(...)</code> builds a WHERE clause on any columns, and you finish it with <code>.first()</code>, <code>.all()</code> or <code>.one()</code>. <code>get(pk)</code> looks up a primary key and can return an object already in the session without a query. In SQLAlchemy 1.4 and later <code>Query.get</code> is legacy, and 2.0 style uses <code>db.session.get(Model, pk)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0052","card":"q-restful-api-015-create-a-planet-post-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s016","q":"In Flask, how do you read a field sent in an HTML form body versus a JSON body, and which one fails on a missing key?","a":"<p>Form data is in <code>request.form</code> and JSON is in <code>request.json</code> (or <code>request.get_json()</code>). Indexing with <code>request.form['key']</code> raises a <code>BadRequestKeyError</code>, which Flask turns into a 400 response. <code>request.form.get('key')</code> returns <code>None</code> instead, so you decide how to handle the absence.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0053","card":"q-restful-api-015-create-a-planet-post-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s016","q":"What status code should a successful POST that creates a resource return, and what else should it ideally send?","a":"<p>201 Created. By convention include a <code>Location</code> header with the new resource's URL and often the created representation in the body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0054","card":"q-restful-api-015-create-a-planet-post-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s016","q":"Where does the <code>@jwt_required</code> decorator go relative to <code>@app.route</code>, and why?","a":"<p><code>@app.route</code> is the outer decorator and the auth decorator sits directly on the function. <code>route</code> registers whatever it receives, so if the auth decorator were outside it, <code>route</code> would register the unprotected function and the check would never run. Version note: in Flask-JWT-Extended 4.0 and later the decorator is called with parentheses, <code>@jwt_required()</code>. The bare form is for 3.x and earlier.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@app.route(&#x27;/items&#x27;, methods=[&#x27;POST&#x27;])\n@jwt_required\ndef add_item():\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0055","card":"q-restful-api-015-create-a-planet-post-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s016","q":"What does a client get when it calls a JWT-protected Flask endpoint without a token, and how does it send one?","a":"<p>It gets 401, with a message that the Authorization header is missing (the exact JSON body depends on the Flask-JWT-Extended version). The client sends <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;access_token&gt;</code>, which in Postman is the Bearer Token auth type.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0056","card":"q-restful-api-015-create-a-planet-post-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s016","q":"Why convert form values with <code>float(...)</code> or <code>int(...)</code> before saving them?","a":"<p>Form values arrive as strings. Storing a string in a numeric column relies on the database to coerce it, and invalid input fails late. Convert and validate in the view (or a schema) so bad input returns 400.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0057","card":"q-restful-api-016-update-a-planet-put-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s017","q":"In SQLAlchemy, why does an update need no <code>db.session.add()</code> call, only <code>commit()</code>?","a":"<p>An object loaded through the session is tracked by it. Changing its attributes marks it as modified, and <code>commit()</code> flushes an UPDATE. <code>add()</code> is only needed for new objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0058","card":"q-restful-api-016-update-a-planet-put-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s017","q":"What is the difference between PUT and PATCH, and what does a PUT handler that only changes some fields risk?","a":"<p>PUT replaces the whole resource with the representation sent, and it should be idempotent. PATCH changes only the fields sent. If a PUT handler reads only some fields and keeps the rest, it behaves like PATCH, and clients that expect full replacement get surprising results. Pick one meaning and document it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0059","card":"q-restful-api-016-update-a-planet-put-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s017","q":"What does 404 versus 200 or 204 mean for an update endpoint, and why return 404 if the ID is missing rather than creating the record?","a":"<p>404 says the resource to update is not there. Creating it silently would hide client mistakes, and PUT-as-upsert is only right when the contract says so. On success return 200 with the resource or 204 with no body. A 202 Accepted means the work is queued but not finished, so it is a poor fit for a synchronous update that is already committed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0060","card":"q-restful-api-016-update-a-planet-put-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s017","q":"An authenticated client calls a protected endpoint and gets \"Token has expired\". What is happening and what should the client do?","a":"<p>Access tokens carry an expiry claim (<code>exp</code>), and the server rejects them after it passes. The client must log in again, or use a refresh token if the API provides one, then retry with the new access token.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0061","card":"q-restful-api-017-delete-a-planet-delete-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s018","q":"How do you delete a row with SQLAlchemy, and what must follow?","a":"<p>Load the object, call <code>db.session.delete(obj)</code>, then <code>db.session.commit()</code>. Without the commit the delete is not persisted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0062","card":"q-restful-api-017-delete-a-planet-delete-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s018","q":"What is the difference between a hard delete and a soft delete, and when do you pick each?","a":"<p>A hard delete removes the row permanently. A soft delete sets a flag such as <code>deleted_at</code> or <code>is_deleted</code> and filters those rows out of queries. Choose soft delete when you need audit history, undo, or to keep references from other tables intact. Choose hard delete when data must really be erased (for example privacy rules) or when history has no value. The cost of soft delete is that every query must remember the filter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0063","card":"q-restful-api-017-delete-a-planet-delete-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s018","q":"Why should DELETE be idempotent, and what does a repeated DELETE return?","a":"<p>The same request repeated should leave the same server state: the resource is gone. The first call returns success (200 or 204). Later calls commonly return 404 because the resource no longer exists. Both are acceptable, and the state is the same.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0064","card":"q-restful-api-017-delete-a-planet-delete-jwt-protected","sec":"restful-api-s018","q":"Why take the ID for a DELETE from the URL path rather than a request body?","a":"<p>The URL identifies the resource, and DELETE semantics are \"remove the resource at this URL\". Many proxies and clients ignore or reject a body on DELETE, so the path is more reliable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0065","card":"q-restful-api-018-status-codes-used-in-the-api","sec":"restful-api-s019","q":"Which status code do you return when a client tries to create a resource that already exists, and why that one?","a":"<p>409 Conflict. The request is syntactically fine, but it conflicts with the current state (a duplicate unique value). 400 means the request itself is invalid, and 422 is used for semantic validation errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0066","card":"q-restful-api-018-status-codes-used-in-the-api","sec":"restful-api-s019","q":"What does 202 Accepted mean, and is it right for a PUT or DELETE that finished immediately?","a":"<p>202 means the server accepted the request for processing but has not completed it, for example when a job is queued. If the update or delete is already done, 200 or 204 is the accurate code, so using 202 misleads clients.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0067","card":"q-restful-api-018-status-codes-used-in-the-api","sec":"restful-api-s019","q":"Why is it a mistake to return 200 with an error message in the body?","a":"<p>Clients, proxies, monitors and retry logic all use the status code. A 200 that hides a failure makes the call look successful, breaks error handling and corrupts metrics. Use the correct 4xx or 5xx code and put details in the body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0068","card":"q-restful-api-019-why-jwt","sec":"restful-api-s020","q":"What is a JSON Web Token and what are its three parts?","a":"<p>A JWT is a signed, compact token that carries claims. It has three Base64URL parts separated by dots: a header (algorithm and type), a payload (claims such as subject and expiry) and a signature over the first two. The payload is encoded, not encrypted, so anyone can read it. Never put secrets in it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0069","card":"q-restful-api-019-why-jwt","sec":"restful-api-s020","q":"What does it mean that JWT authentication keeps an API stateless, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>The server stores no session. Each request carries a signed token, and the server verifies the signature and expiry. That makes it easy to scale across several servers. The trade-off is that a token cannot be revoked easily before it expires, unless you add a deny list, which brings back state, so use short lifetimes and refresh tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0070","card":"q-restful-api-019-why-jwt","sec":"restful-api-s020","q":"What is the difference between authentication and authorization, and which does a JWT cover?","a":"<p>Authentication proves who the caller is (login). Authorization decides what that caller may do. A JWT carries identity from authentication, and claims such as roles can feed authorization checks, but the server must still enforce them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0071","card":"q-restful-api-019-why-jwt","sec":"restful-api-s020","q":"When would you choose server-side sessions with cookies over JWT?","a":"<p>For a browser-only app on one domain, server-side sessions allow immediate logout and revocation and keep nothing sensitive on the client. JWT fits APIs consumed by mobile, single-page and third-party clients, and services that must scale without shared session storage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0072","card":"q-restful-api-019-why-jwt","sec":"restful-api-s020","q":"How is the signature of a JWT used, and what happens if a client changes the payload?","a":"<p>The server computes the signature with its secret key (HMAC such as HS256) or a private key (RSA, ECDSA) over header and payload. If the payload changes, the signature no longer matches and verification fails, so the token is rejected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0073","card":"q-restful-api-020-register-a-user-post-open","sec":"restful-api-s021","q":"Why is a registration endpoint open (no token), and what should it validate?","a":"<p>A new user has no credentials yet, so requiring a token makes registration impossible. Because anyone can call it, validate the email format, enforce password rules, reject duplicates with 409, and add rate limiting to slow abuse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0074","card":"q-restful-api-021-jwt-setup","sec":"restful-api-s022","q":"In Flask-JWT-Extended, what do <code>JWTManager(app)</code> and the <code>JWT_SECRET_KEY</code> setting do?","a":"<p><code>JWTManager(app)</code> wires the extension into the Flask app so decorators such as <code>jwt_required</code> and functions such as <code>create_access_token</code> work. <code>JWT_SECRET_KEY</code> is the key used to sign and verify tokens. Anyone who knows it can forge tokens, so use a long random value loaded from an environment variable, never hard-coded in source.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask_jwt_extended import JWTManager\n\napp.config[&#x27;JWT_SECRET_KEY&#x27;] = &#x27;change-me&#x27;\njwt = JWTManager(app)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0075","card":"q-restful-api-021-jwt-setup","sec":"restful-api-s022","q":"Which Flask-JWT-Extended pieces do you import to protect a route and to issue a token?","a":"<p><code>jwt_required</code> protects a route, <code>create_access_token</code> makes the token at login, and <code>JWTManager</code> initializes the extension. <code>get_jwt_identity</code> reads the identity of the current caller inside a protected view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0076","card":"q-restful-api-021-jwt-setup","sec":"restful-api-s022","q":"What happens to all issued tokens when you change the JWT signing key?","a":"<p>They stop verifying, because the signature no longer matches, so every user must log in again. That makes rotating the key a way to invalidate all tokens at once, and it is also why a changed key in one of several servers causes random 401s.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0077","card":"q-restful-api-022-login-post-open-issues-the-token","sec":"restful-api-s023","q":"How does a login endpoint in Flask-JWT-Extended issue a token, and what does it return on bad credentials?","a":"<p>It looks up the user, checks the password, and on success calls <code>create_access_token(identity=...)</code> and returns the token with 200. On failure it returns 401 with a message. The message is the same for unknown email and wrong password, so it does not reveal which one was wrong. <code>check_password</code> here stands for a hash comparison such as <code>werkzeug.security.check_password_hash</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask import request, jsonify\nfrom flask_jwt_extended import create_access_token\n\n@app.route(&#x27;/login&#x27;, methods=[&#x27;POST&#x27;])\ndef login():\n    email = request.json[&#x27;email&#x27;]\n    password = request.json[&#x27;password&#x27;]\n    user = User.query.filter_by(email=email).first()\n    if user and user.check_password(password):\n        token = create_access_token(identity=email)\n        return jsonify(access_token=token), 200\n    return jsonify(message=&#x27;bad email or password&#x27;), 401</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0078","card":"q-restful-api-022-login-post-open-issues-the-token","sec":"restful-api-s023","q":"Why query by email and password together in one filter (as simple tutorials do) is a bad design?","a":"<p>It only works if passwords are stored in plain text, and it compares secrets in SQL. Look up the user by email, then compare the submitted password with the stored hash using a constant-time hash check function.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0079","card":"q-restful-api-022-login-post-open-issues-the-token","sec":"restful-api-s023","q":"How does a Flask view tell whether the request body is JSON or form data, and why support both?","a":"<p>Check <code>request.is_json</code>, then read <code>request.json</code>, otherwise read <code>request.form</code>. Supporting both lets API clients send JSON and simple HTML forms or Postman form-data work too. In a strict API, pick one and validate it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0080","card":"q-restful-api-022-login-post-open-issues-the-token","sec":"restful-api-s023","q":"How does a client use the access token after login?","a":"<p>It sends the header <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> with each request to a protected endpoint. The server checks the signature and expiry and, if valid, runs the view and can read the caller's identity from the token.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0081","card":"q-restful-api-022-login-post-open-issues-the-token","sec":"restful-api-s023","q":"Why use POST for login rather than GET with credentials in the query string?","a":"<p>A GET URL is stored in browser history, server logs and proxy logs, so credentials in it leak. POST puts them in the body, which is not logged by default, and it is used over HTTPS so they are encrypted in transit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0082","card":"q-restful-api-022-login-post-open-issues-the-token","sec":"restful-api-s023","q":"Where should the client keep the access token, and what is the risk of each place in a browser?","a":"<p><code>localStorage</code> is readable by any script on the page, so an XSS bug steals the token. An <code>HttpOnly</code> cookie is hidden from scripts but, when the browser sends it automatically, needs CSRF protection. Short token lifetimes limit the damage in both cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0083","card":"q-restful-api-023-email-with-flask-mail-lost-password","sec":"restful-api-s024","q":"In Flask-Mail, what do <code>Mail(app)</code>, <code>Message</code> and <code>mail.send</code> each do?","a":"<p><code>Mail(app)</code> creates the mail extension configured from <code>app.config</code> (<code>MAIL_SERVER</code>, <code>MAIL_PORT</code>, <code>MAIL_USERNAME</code>, <code>MAIL_PASSWORD</code>, TLS settings). <code>Message</code> describes the email (subject, sender, recipients, body). <code>mail.send(msg)</code> sends it through the SMTP server. The <code>Mail</code> object must be configured before the endpoint runs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask_mail import Mail, Message\n\nmail = Mail(app)\nmsg = Message(&#x27;Subject&#x27;, sender=&#x27;noreply@example.com&#x27;, recipients=[&#x27;user@example.com&#x27;])\nmail.send(msg)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"restful-api-f0084","card":"q-restful-api-023-email-with-flask-mail-lost-password","sec":"restful-api-s024","q":"Why use a service such as Mailtrap while developing email features?","a":"<p>It is a fake SMTP server that catches outgoing messages in an inbox instead of delivering them. You can inspect headers, HTML and text safely, and there is no risk of mailing real users from a test environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0085","card":"q-restful-api-023-email-with-flask-mail-lost-password","sec":"restful-api-s024","q":"Why is emailing a user their password a security flaw, and what is the right password-reset flow?","a":"<p>Sending a password means it is stored recoverably (not hashed) and travels through email in readable form. The right flow: the user requests a reset, the server creates a random, single-use token with a short expiry, stores only its hash, emails a link containing the token, and the user sets a new password through that link. Also rate-limit the endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0086","card":"q-restful-api-023-email-with-flask-mail-lost-password","sec":"restful-api-s024","q":"What should a \"forgot password\" endpoint return when the email is not registered?","a":"<p>Return the same response and status as for a registered email (for example 200 with \"if the account exists, an email was sent\"). A different answer lets attackers discover which emails have accounts. The trade-off is less specific feedback for mistyped addresses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"restful-api-f0087","card":"q-restful-api-023-email-with-flask-mail-lost-password","sec":"restful-api-s024","q":"Why should email sending in a web request often move to a background job?","a":"<p>SMTP calls are slow and can fail, so doing them inside the request makes the response slow and ties it to mail-server problems. A task queue such as Celery lets the endpoint return quickly and retry failed sends.</p>","hand":true}];
